      "how_your_choices_affect_cost": "💡 How your choices affect cost:",
      "humped_low_clearance_crossing": "Humped / low-clearance crossing",
      "hybrid_city_highway_mpg_because_regene": "• Hybrid city > highway MPG because regenerative braking recovers energy in stop-and-go. Gas cars do the opposite.",
      "hydraulic_fluid_transmits_pedal_force_": "Hydraulic fluid transmits pedal force to calipers. Incompressible = instant response. Absorbs moisture over time.",
      "hydraulic_or_electric_assist_that_redu": "Hydraulic or electric assist that reduces steering effort. Without it, turning at low speed requires enormous force.",
      "hydroplane": "Hydroplane",
      "hydroplane_loss_of_control": "Hydroplane Loss of Control",
      "hydroplane_speed_threshold": "Hydroplane speed threshold",
      "hypermiling_content": "Hypermiling is the art of squeezing every mile from a gallon. Proven techniques: (1) Gentle accel — imagine an egg between your foot and the pedal. (2) Anticipate — read the road 15 seconds ahead so you brake less. (3) Coast to stops — fuel cut-off activates when you lift off the gas, meaning coasting in gear uses ZERO fuel on modern cars. (4) Pulse and glide — gently accelerate, then coast. Works best under 45 mph. (5) Highway cruise at 55-60 mph, not 75 (drag is brutal). (6) Tires at door-sticker PSI. (7) Remove the roof box when not using it. (8) Don't idle — modern engines restart on <0.5 s of fuel, so 30+ sec of idling wastes more than restarting. Combined, these techniques can boost real-world MPG by 20-40% over aggressive driving. Free money.",
      "hypermiling_lab_2": "Hypermiling Lab",
      "hypermiling_lab_3": "Hypermiling Lab",
      "hypothesis_free_text_why_does_kinetic_": "Hypothesis (free text): Why does kinetic energy grow with v² but only linearly with mass?",
      "i_295_merge_65_mph_3_second_following_": "I-295 merge. 65 mph, 3-second following, smooth acceleration.",
      "i_can_beat_that_train": "\"I can beat that train\"",
      "i_only_had_two_beers_i_m_totally_fine_": "\"I only had two beers, I'm totally fine to drive. You're overreacting.\"",
      "i_understand_explain_in_own_words": "I understand — explain in own words",
      "i_will_always_wear_my_seatbelt_i_will_": "I will always wear my seatbelt. I will not start the car until every passenger is belted in.",
      "i_will_be_kind_to_other_drivers_even_w": "I will be kind to other drivers — even when they aren't kind to me. Road rage has no winners.",
      "i_will_come_to_a_complete_stop_at_ever": "I will come to a complete stop at every stop sign. I will never run a yellow light.",
      "i_will_keep_3_seconds_of_following_dis": "I will keep 3 seconds of following distance in clear weather, 4 seconds in rain, 6+ seconds in snow or fog.",
      "i_will_never_drive_impaired_not_by_alc": "I will never drive impaired — not by alcohol, not by cannabis, not by medication that warns against it, not by lack of sleep.",
      "i_will_never_text_call_or_touch_my_pho": "I will never text, call, or touch my phone while driving. Notifications wait. Texts wait. The deer will not.",
      "i_will_pull_to_the_right_and_stop_for_": "I will pull to the right and stop for emergency vehicles. I will slow down and move over for roadside workers.",
      "i_will_scan_my_mirrors_every_5_8_secon": "I will scan my mirrors every 5-8 seconds. I will look 15 seconds down the road. I will shoulder-check before every lane change.",
      "i_will_slow_down_for_school_zones_cons": "I will slow down for school zones, construction zones, and anywhere I see people.",
      "ice": "🧊 Ice",
      "ice_0_1_7_dry_off_this_chart": "Ice (μ=0.1) ≈ 7× dry — off this chart.",
      "ice_2": "🧊 Ice",
      "ice_vis_looks_clear": "looks clear!",
      "ideal_tire_model_note": "Ideal-tire model: braking distance here depends on speed and friction only. Real heavy vehicles stop longer (brake fade, tire loading) — see Vehicle Comparison.",
      "if_impact_is_unavoidable": "If impact is unavoidable",
      "if_right_shoulder_is_blocked_stop_wher": " If the right shoulder is blocked, signal and move as far right as practicable, stay clear of intersections, and stop where the lane stays clearest.",
      "if_the_emergency_vehicle_is_on_the_oth": " Maine §2054 does not list a median exception; move right when safe, stay clear of intersections, and stop until it passes.",
      "if_the_examiner_has_to_verbally_correc": "If the examiner has to verbally correct you to prevent danger = fail.",
      "if_you_miss_your_exit_go_around_again_": "If you miss your exit, go around again. That is the beauty of a roundabout — no penalty for looping.",
      "if_you_re_inside_an_intersection_when_": "If you're inside an intersection when you hear a siren, CLEAR the intersection first, then pull right. Stopping in the intersection blocks the emergency vehicle's path.",
      "if_you_stall_on_the_tracks": "If you stall on the tracks",
      "impact_angle": "Impact angle",
      "impact_speed": "Impact speed",
      "impaired_0_08_bac": "Modeled impairment",
      "implied_consent": "Implied consent",
      "important": "Important",
      "in_a_multi_lane_roundabout_right_lane_": "In a multi-lane roundabout: right lane = first exit. Left lane = second or third exit.",
      "in_any_real_emergency": "📞 In any real emergency",
      "in_moose_country_and_zero_oncoming_tra": "In moose country and zero oncoming traffic, use high beams. A moose's eyes don't always reflect like a deer's, but its body outline appears sooner. Dim for oncoming cars, then pop them back on.",
      "in_the_u_s_all_roundabouts_circulate_c": "In the U.S., all roundabouts circulate counterclockwise. You enter to the right and exit to the right. Signal right as you approach your exit.",
      "incorrect_why_this_matters": "✗ INCORRECT — WHY THIS MATTERS",
      "inertia_content": "Every driving decision is physics. 1st Law (inertia): a body in motion stays in motion. That is why you need brakes — to fight your car's inertia. It is also why loose objects become missiles in a crash. 2nd Law (F = ma): force = mass × acceleration. A heavier car takes more force to stop. 3rd Law (action/reaction): when your engine pushes on the tires, the road pushes back — that reaction force is what actually moves the car forward. Without friction (ice), the wheels spin but the car goes nowhere, because there is no reaction force. Understanding these three laws means you can predict how your car will behave in any situation before it surprises you.",
      "inflate_in_30_milliseconds_during_a_cr": "Inflate in ~30 milliseconds during a crash. Front, side, curtain, knee. SRS light on = system malfunction — get checked.",
      "inside_a_work_zone_a_flagger_s_instruc": "Inside a work zone, a flagger's instructions override every other traffic control device — including traffic lights. Flagger STOP paddle means stop even if your light is green.",
      "inside_each_wheel_measures_air_pressur": "Inside each wheel, measures air pressure. Light comes on if 25%+ below recommended. Check ALL 4 tires when it lights.",
