      "midnight_black": "Midnight Black",
      "mixed_20_qs": "Mixed, 20 Qs",
      "moose": "Moose",
      "moose_brake": "Moose: brake ",
      "moose_crossing_with_oncoming_traffic": "Moose crossing with oncoming traffic",
      "moose_encounter_drill": "Moose Encounter Drill",
      "moose_encounter_drill_2": "Moose Encounter Drill",
      "moose_in_the_lane_at_dusk": "Moose in the lane at dusk",
      "moose_warnings_black_ice_winter_kit_st": "Moose warnings, black ice, winter kit, studded tires.",
      "most_crashes_are_i_didn_t_see_them": "Most crashes are \"I didn't see them\"",
      "most_drivers_set_side_mirrors_to_see_t": "Most drivers set side mirrors to see the back of their own car — that doubles the rearview mirror. Instead: LEAN LEFT until your head almost touches the window, then adjust the left mirror until you just see your car's side. Repeat on the right by leaning right. When sitting normally, you'll see the lanes beside you — ELIMINATING BLIND SPOTS.",
      "motorcycles_and_bicycles_can_hide_in_b": "• Motorcycles and bicycles can hide in blind spots that even cars cannot.",
      "motorcycles_fit_entirely_in_your_a_pil": "Motorcycles fit ENTIRELY in your A-pillar blind spot (the pillar between windshield and door). Bikes + cyclists do the same. Always lean to see past pillars at intersections.",
      "motorcyclists_swerve_for_reasons": "Motorcyclists swerve for reasons",
      "move_over": "Move over:",
      "move_over_law": "Move Over law",
      "move_to_the_right_lane_stay_at_65": "Move to the right lane, stay at 65",
      "mpg_by_speed_chart_label": "Miles per gallon by speed chart",
      "mpg_content": "Miles per gallon (MPG) is a ratio — distance divided by fuel used. Higher = better. But MPG hides something important: the relationship is NON-LINEAR. Going from 15 to 20 MPG saves more fuel than going from 35 to 40 MPG, even though both are a 5 MPG improvement. That is why gallons-per-100-miles (GPM) is sometimes a better metric. Europe uses L/100km for exactly this reason. The things that move your MPG up or down are: speed (sweet spot 45-55 mph), aggression (jackrabbit starts = -15 to -40%), tire pressure (low = -3 to -10%), AC use (-5 to -10% at low speed, negligible at highway), roof racks (-10 to -25%), cargo weight (-1% per 100 lbs), and engine condition.",
      "mpg_vs_speed_graph_beat_the_epa_rating": "MPG vs speed graph — beat the EPA rating.",
